# Build output. Regenerated by `dotnet build`, so it must never be committed — tracking it meant every
# build dirtied the tree and buried real diffs under a thousand changed .dll and .pdb files.
bin/
obj/

# Visual Studio / Rider / VS Code
.vs/
.idea/
*.user
*.suo
*.userprefs
.vscode/

# Test and coverage output
TestResults/
*.coverage
*.coveragexml

# NuGet
*.nupkg
packages/

# OS noise
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store

# Code written by `protodesigner generate`. The .pdproj is the source of truth; emitted headers are build
# output like any other.
generated/
out/

# NOTE: samples/ was ignored here, which meant samples/telemetry.pdproj — a hand-maintained fixture the
# README and the CLI tests both point at — was never tracked. It is source, and it is tracked now.

# The protobuf toolchain used by ProtocConformanceTests: bin/protoc, include/ (the well-known types),
# and Buf's validate.proto. Not vendored — protoc alone is a 12 MB platform-specific binary that git
# would keep forever, and the C cross-check already treats MSVC the same way: find it, or fail with
# instructions. Lay it out as protobuf/{bin,include,validate.proto}, or set PROTODESIGNER_PROTOC.
#
# The protovalidate runtime check needs nothing here. Its Go harness and go.mod/go.sum are tracked under
# tests/ProtoDesigner.CodeGen.Tests/Protovalidate/, and its dependencies come from Go's global module
# cache — so this whole directory can be deleted and rebuilt from downloads at any time.
protobuf/
